1. Research question
Does genetically predicted liability to loneliness causally influence the risk of clinically diagnosed Alzheimer’s disease?

2. Hypothesis
H0:β_loneliness→AD=0
H1:β_loneliness→AD=0

3. Data
Exposure: Day et al. 2018 loneliness GWAS
Outcome: Kunkle et al. 2019 diagnosed AD GWAS
European ancestry

4. Instrument selection:
P<5×10^−8
540 candidate SNPs
LD clumping: r^2 <0.001, 10,000 kb
13 independent SNPs
F-statistics all >10
2 palindromic ambiguous SNPs removed during harmonisation
11 SNPs entered MR

5. Main MR results
IVW: OR 0.64, 95% CI 0.10–4.01, P=0.631
Weighted median: OR 0.52, P=0.593
MR-Egger: very imprecise, P=0.133

6. Sensitivity analyses
IVW heterogeneity: Q=10.60, P=0.389
Egger intercept: −0.043, P=0.107
Leave-one-out: no single SNP dominated
Single-SNP estimates: broad and imprecise

7. Interpretation
No evidence of a causal effect
But estimates are imprecise, so conclusion is inconclusive rather than evidence of no effect

8. Figures
MR scatter plot
Single-SNP forest plot
Leave-one-out plot